assertion  XSH.round.001
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For arguments of the three floating types (float, double, long double)
           that are -100.0625, -3.5, -2.75, -2.5, -1.5, -1.25, -0.75, 0.75, 1.25,
           1.5, 2.5, 2.75, 3, -3, 3.5 and 100.0625, round() returns the nearest
           integer, halfway cases away from zero.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.round.002
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For arguments 2^p and -2^p (p being the number of digits of the type),
           which are integral, round() returns the argument itself.
test       t/002-large-integral.c

assertion  XSH.round.003
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For -0.5, -0.25, 0.25 and 0.5 the result has the same sign as the
           argument, even when it is zero.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-sign.c

assertion  XSH.round.004
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       A NaN argument gives a NaN result.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-nan.c

assertion  XSH.round.005
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For +0 and -0 the result is the argument, with its sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-zero.c

assertion  XSH.round.006
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For +Inf and -Inf the result is the argument.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.round.007
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, round(2.5) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/007-no-error.c

assertion  XSH.round.008
anchor     functions/round.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/round.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), round() of -2.5, -1.5, -0.5, 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, -2.75 and 2.75
           gives -3, -2, -1, 1, 2, 3, -3 and 3.
test       t/008-rounding-mode.c
